    <sense id="b452249d-5017-4d97-9f0d-447aed630bf3">
      <grammatical-info value="Verbo">
        <trait name="type" value="inflAffix"/>
        <trait name="Verbo-slot" value="obj"/>
        <trait name="Verbo-slot" value="sbj"/>
      </grammatical-info>
      <gloss lang="en">
        <text>2P</text>
      </gloss>
      <gloss lang="pt">
        <text>2P</text>
      </gloss>
      <definition>
        <form lang="en">
          <text>second person plural, "you"</text>
        </form>
        <form lang="pt">
          <text>segunda pessoa plural, "vocês"</text>
        </form>
      </definition>
      <note>
        <form lang="en">
          <text>Question: Are there any examples of mw/ _ [V]? Question: 0 / _ [nasal] | how to handle nullsdefine bilabial and stops; May require a mcc +/ [tense] ... _ to prevent some ambiguities; Question: what is it before a vowel?; a m / _ [labial] / _ [OU] | eg. kumpa kumona kumolota kumulaya; a n / _ [C] | eg. kunsowera</text>
        </form>
      </note>
      <note type="phonology">
        <form lang="en">
          <text>alt: <span lang="seh">mw / _ [V] |fails for PART: mbamuapfunzisa; mu / _ [C]; mu +/ PART..._; mu +/ _ {monosyllabic} | eg. munthu; mw / _ [V] | eg. mwana mwimbi *mwonsense; m' / _ [labialaspirated] | eg. m'bale; m / _ [labial] | eg. mpfunzisi; n' / _ [nonlabialaspirated] | eg. n'th??; n / _ [C] | eg. nkolakazi nzungu nrimi; mu; m' / _ [labialaspirated]; m / _ [labial] |eg pi-da-m-menye ka-m-bhoma; n' / _ [nonlabialaspirated] |eg. ka-n'-khucha; n / _ [C] |eg ka-n-tema (cortar); mu / _ [V] |eg pi-da-mu-ona; mu |eg amulamusa</span></text>
        </form>
      </note>
      <note type="source">
        <form lang="en">
          <text>Schadeberg 2.1.3.8</text>
        </form>
      </note>
      <field type="Parsing Note">
        <form lang="en">
          <text>This is used as a nominalizer as well kutumwa &gt; m'tumwa/m'tumwi; Seem that there are times that the m' rule does not apply. Why?</text>
        </form>
      </field>
    </sense>
  </entry>
